N('wareham-opera-house-manhattan','Wareham Opera House (Wareham Hall)','Manhattan','ks','civic','standing',39.17970,-96.56235,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1884–1910',
  'A theatre on a lot used for shows since the Coliseum of about 1882. H.S. Moore built a stone opera house here in 1884; H.P. Wareham bought it in 1893, and the present front, inscribed "Wareham 1910", came with its rebuilding in 1909 and 1910. The marquee was put up in 1938. In 2022 two nonprofit groups were formed to buy and renovate it, and they say it will reopen as a performance and events hall by winter 2027.',
  look='A two-storey front of tan brick with carved stone trim: three bays of paired windows with stained glass transoms, a carved shield with a "W" between them, and a balustraded parapet. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Wareham Hall Inc., a nonprofit, owns it and is renovating it (2026). Seen from Poyntz Avenue. '+DTN,
  links=[DT,['Wareham Hall: about','https://warehamhall.org/about']],
  brick='no',hours=''),
N('wareham-hotel-manhattan','Wareham Hotel','Manhattan','ks','hotel','standing',39.17969,-96.56255,'exact','view','walk','',
  '1925–1926',
  'A six-storey steel-framed hotel begun in 1925 and finished in 1926 by H.P. Wareham, who meant it as the city\'s first skyscraper. It had 150 rooms over a lobby and dining rooms, with apartments and Wareham\'s own home on the top floors. The 1951 flood ruined much of its furniture, and in the 1980s it was turned into apartments.',
  look='Brown brick with classical terra cotta trim, balconies on scrolled brackets, a cornice band inscribed "H.P. Wareham 1926" and a balustrade along the top; tan terra cotta and granite at street level. Interior not checked.',
  accn='Apartments upstairs, with a restaurant on the ground floor in 2016; seen from Poyntz Avenue. '+DTN,
  links=[DT,['Clio: The Historic Wareham Hotel','https://theclio.com/entry/27363']],
  brick='no',hours=''),
